culate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 11 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-275521/field-1" "q-275521/field-2" "q-275521/field-3" "q-275521/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-275521"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943/field-1/57a3ae1161af9413554a1cb9218656e1f4442c31b5d01877864c58878ea984a5" :answer/id #uuid "25dcc8cc-c670-5538-ae5a-f2183393a9ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "78" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfde15d-c0d4-5bd5-ab89-6a3066399132" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-319943/field-1/57a3ae1161af9413554a1cb9218656e1f4442c31b5d01877864c58878ea984a5" :answer-field/choices ["q-319943/field-1/57a3ae1161af9413554a1cb9218656e1f4442c31b5d01877864c58878ea984a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943/field-2/5d75fc115d122bcf89c08ce3361f5bf132566cb78a3191c8592f2028be7e68b0" :answer/id #uuid "67e27f0c-3baf-5ac2-bf27-0a97620611a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea841dd-755f-539e-b9f4-c7f1030784c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-319943/field-2/5d75fc115d122bcf89c08ce3361f5bf132566cb78a3191c8592f2028be7e68b0" :answer-field/choices ["q-319943/field-2/5d75fc115d122bcf89c08ce3361f5bf132566cb78a3191c8592f2028be7e68b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943/field-3/207ccf8ec26a180258f83c8f2f2a64095ad864354653ecc2f10bffa08cb7c1df" :answer/id #uuid "406612ae-4c02-519c-9e3a-0c3529b9c23e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-182" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "776e12a2-58df-5d71-86df-ce9117b18ca6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-319943/field-3/207ccf8ec26a180258f83c8f2f2a64095ad864354653ecc2f10bffa08cb7c1df" :answer-field/choices ["q-319943/field-3/207ccf8ec26a180258f83c8f2f2a64095ad864354653ecc2f10bffa08cb7c1df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-319943" :question/id #uuid "78c35ecd-2c3f-5a39-9862-6094c63c5273" :question/math-academy-id "q-319943" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-20-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}78 \\\\ -52 \\\\ -182\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-319943/field-1" "q-319943/field-2" "q-319943/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-319943"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
